          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "101"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.114,
            0.083,
            0.744,
            0.16
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Since water scarcity makes life difficult, people have developed different ways to conserve water. For example, in Ladakh, people have developed innovative ways to conserve water by making ice stupas (Fig. 7.13) during the winters."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.144,
            0.187,
            0.387,
            0.207
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "SCIENCE AND SOCIETY"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.145,
            0.213,
            0.246,
            0.231
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ice Stupa"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.143,
            0.234,
            0.408,
            0.47
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "During the spring season in Ladakh, streams often dry up, leading to scarcity of water as the heat from the Sun’s radiation is not enough to melt the snow on the mountains. During winters, water from mountain streams is channeled down through underground pipes. This water is then sprayed into the cold air. As it falls, it freezes due to extremely low temperatures."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.415,
            0.211,
            0.815,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 5,
              "bbox": [
                0.415,
                0.211,
                0.815,
                0.418
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_5.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.529,
                0.42,
                0.682,
                0.438
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 7.13: Ice stupa"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.142,
            0.469,
            0.76,
            0.54
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The ice builds up layer by layer, creating a tall, cone-shaped structure called an "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "ice stupa",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " as shown in Fig. 7.13. The ice stupa melts slowly during spring, providing water for farming and other needs throughout the summer."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.77,
            0.429,
            0.899,
            0.544
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 8,
              "bbox": [
                0.77,
                0.429,
                0.899,
                0.544
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_8.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.574,
            0.309,
            0.595
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In a Nutshell"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.621,
            0.743,
            0.661
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "There are three ways in which heat is transferred from one place to another — conduction, convection, and radiation."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.664,
            0.744,
            0.721
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The process of heat transfer from the hotter part of an object to a colder part is called conduction. In this process, particles do not move from their positions."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.778,
            0.622,
            0.923,
            0.733
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 12,
              "bbox": [
                0.778,
                0.622,
                0.923,
                0.733
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_12_image_12.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.725,
            0.743,
            0.763
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Materials that allow heat to pass through them easily are called good conductors of heat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.767,
            0.743,
            0.806
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Materials that do not allow heat to pass through them easily are called poor conductors (insulators) of heat."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.809,
            0.744,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In solids, heat is mainly transferred through the process of conduction. In liquids and gases, heat is transferred by the process of convection."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "aside_text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.959,
            0.119,
            0.979,
            0.279
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Heat Transfer in Nature"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
